A||N||12||b lo 0||PLEASE GOD HELP ME!!!!!||||1||
Z||000000||b lo 0||06-22-2001||02:06 AM||||I have posted before on this- My beloved is still popping fuel pump fuses when too much gas is applied suddenly, and refuses to rev past about 4500 rpm.  Computer says bad signal from distributor.  I have replaced ignition module, fuel pump, fuel filter, bobbin coil (in distributor), and have de-corrosionized (is that a real word?) the entire engine assembly.  I am about to clean the injectors (outside of engine) and check the fuel rail.  Has ANYBODY experienced ANYTHING even remotely close to this before?  I am lost, as is my dad (mechanic) and everyone I ask.  The computer was replaced before I bought the money pit...umm...I mean joy of my life.  BTW, I missed you guys in my repeated hiatus!  1fst2m6, I PROMISE to make a GA Fieros Club meeting in this lifetime!  Please help me with this!!  BTW, eng. is 2.8l with auto trans-thnx.<P>------------------<BR>'85 GT (in progress)  <BR>titled ICE-plates B LO 0 <BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.angelfire.com/ga3/audioimagery/ice.gif">     <BR>Go to <A HREF="http://www.audioimagery.i-p.com" TARGET=_blank>www.audioimagery.i-p.com</A>  !!!<P><A HREF="http://clubs.excite.com/welcome/main?cid=.zXb1DJ1lsCL&aid=22&auth=" TARGET=_blank>Pontiac Fiero Owner's Club</A>-Pics, Videos and More-Or Post your Own||||reg||1
Z||000001||Oreif||06-22-2001||02:23 AM||||Fuse popping ~ bad wire(s) or corroded connector(s)?<P>You can ohm out from the fuse to the connector on the outside of the tank. Anything higher than 2 ohms I would start checking connectors.<P>Stupid response ~ Is it the right value fuse?<P>Low RPM ~ Clogged Catalytic Converter? <BR>Tap it with a hammer, if it sounds like a league ball is loose inside, It's bad. You could also remove it and see if you can see light thru it. no light = clogged.<P><p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 06-22-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Ken Wittlief||06-22-2001||09:30 AM||||If the fuse for the fuel pump blows when you step down on the gas - then there has to be a cause and effect relationship there.<BR>  <BR>  since the fuel pump runs at a steady rate and doens not know what the gas pedal postion is - it must be something like:<P>  1. engine moves on its mounts enough to pinch the fuel pump wire somewhere and short it out?<P>  2. the fuel pump wire is somewhere near the throttle pedal or cable - when the pedal is in a certain position the wire is shrorted to ground?<P>  3. The fuel pump wire is running through the frame somewhere - maybe outside its protective grommet - when the car is accelerating hard the frame flexes just enough to pinch the wire and short it.<P>  4. the fuel pump is loose inside the tank - hard aceleration causes it to move to one side, shorting out the wire to it (that would be bad!)<P>  it sounds like you need to trace out the wire from the fuse box to the fuel pump and make sure it is not shorting anywhere in between. <P>   also make sure someone didnt add on another wire to the fuel pump fuse (stereo, amp, foglight) or didnt move a headlite wire or something else onto the same fuse.<P>  fuses blow when they are overloaded or when the connected wire is shorted to ground - no other reason. If you are confident your fuel pump is not drawing more than its suppose to, then it has to be one of the things I mentioned above.<P>  BTW - Im not God, but I play Him on TV (c%||||reg||1
Z||000003||JetroGT||06-22-2001||09:34 AM||||Something like that happened to a guy from New York two years ago when he drove from NY to the Dells run. He just had his pump replaced but the guys at the shop didnt put enough shielding on the wires leading to the pump. So every time he accelereated or hit a bump, he poped a fuse. His car was an 88 GT, if that matters.||||reg||1
Z||000004||84Bill||06-22-2001||09:41 AM||||Thats a toughy, Do what the others recomended as far as looking over the wires also it might be worthwile to see how much juice the pump is drawing. If there is a short in the field wire it will draw to many amps and pop the fuse.<BR>BTW I have a pump if you need one, say 20 bucks + ship?<p>[This message has been edited by 84Bill (edited 06-22-2001).]||||reg||14
Z||000005||Old Lar||06-22-2001||10:13 AM||||I had the 88 with a fuel pump popping fuse.  I had had an engine swap and when the wiring harness from the engine to the car was hooked back up, the harness was routed over the 88 heat exhaust shield which has a sharp edge.  The hot sharp edge melted a wire in the harness and periodically as I accelerated, the engine would move and the wire would short out on the heat shield, blowing the fuse. When I left the Dells, I went through 5 fuses to the gas station.  No way to go 800 miles back home.<P>What was odd, was the wire harness was the big harness on the left side of the engine back by the firewall, not the small harness from the oil sending unit/ fuel pump harness.<P>I'd like to take credit for the discovery, but Bob Steiger at Twin Lakes, trailered the car to his shop and after looking at it for a while saw the routing problem.  The fix was to reroute the harness and retape the harness with some heat resistant tape.  Problem went away.  <P>But somewhere in your wire harness, there is a short in the fuel pump circuit.<P>Good Luck!||||reg||1
Z||000006||b lo 0||06-22-2001||12:29 PM||||Thanks for the great responses.  I'll check the current demand next.  The cat is also very new, and all the wiring has been checked-I even replaced the entire bundle which goes over the driver's side exhaust heat shield as in Old Lar's car.  84Bill, I appreciate the offer on the pump, but mine is brand new (all $70 worth).  Thanks again for the help!||||reg||1
Z||000007||Old Lar||06-22-2001||01:08 PM||||Make sure the wiring bundle goes behind the heat shield, rather than over it.  That is where mine was shorting.  My wiring bundle is behind the heat shield along the firewall.<BR>My one concern would be if that same thing has happened on your car, could two wires have had the insulation cut and they are shorting out against each other.  <P>Electrical problems are a real pain.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Toddster||06-22-2001||01:28 PM||||I know you have already replaced the fuel pump but let me point-out that electric fuel pumps are really twitchy where heat is concerned. <BR>I had a bad fuel pump once (cooked do to heat), I replaced it but did not put new heat sheilding around it. The new pump went bad within 80 miles!<P>How is your heat sheilding?||||reg||1
Z||000009||b lo 0||06-22-2001||05:50 PM||||Toddster-you forget, on Fiero's the fuel pump is inside the fuel tank, a good 3 feet from the nearest engine part.  I don't think heat is the problem here.  I'm gonna replace the CPU and see if that does it.  Thanks again, guys.||||reg||1
Z||000010||theogre||06-22-2001||08:29 PM||||A bad relay or a bad oil pressure switch are also posibilities for a fuel pump power problem. <P>It's also posible the new pump is bad. <P>There's not much on that circuit to go bad.<P>I'd check over the engine wiring really carefully. It's real easy to have a wire partly melted that only makes a short under specific conditions. Like when the motor shifts on it's mounts durring hard acceleration or braking.||||reg||1
Z||000011||b lo 0||06-23-2001||12:23 PM||||The relay is good, checked it out, and the oil pressure sensor is new.  The entire wiring harness has been checked out, so I'm going to resort to the computer-it's all I haven't replaced.  Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000012||theogre||06-23-2001||12:48 PM||||The computer would not cause the pump fuse to blow. You'd fry the computer before the fuse went and that would require a short in the relay wires.<P>check all the wire again anyway. The kind of thing you are deiscribing points to Something is moving and pinching a wire and/or pulling a connector appart.<P>As I said above... Ther is very little on the fuel pump circuit. It's one of the simplest circuits in the whole car.<P>If the fuel pump is having a power problem it can cause part of the other problems.<P>The ECM could be bad, causing the spark error, but you need to eliminate that fuel power problem. <p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 06-23-2001).]||||reg||1
